Images
------
All question images are documented individually in images/Bildnachweis.md, with
source, author and licence for each. Summary:

  * official coats of arms and a flag — public domain (§5 UrhG), no conditions
  * two base maps — CC BY-SA 3.0, attributed, composites licensed CC BY-SA 4.0
  * two photographs — CC BY-SA 4.0, attributed (Reichstag; Plenarsaal)
  * two illustrations — drawn for this project, CC BY-SA 4.0

Three questions in the catalogue are illustrated with period press photographs
that remain in copyright. No freely licensed photograph of the same scene
exists, and substituting a different picture would make the question text
describe something the image does not show — so those images were removed and
the questions stand on their text alone. See the Bildnachweis for which ones.

If you believe an image here is misattributed or should not be included, open
an issue naming the file, and it will be corrected or removed promptly.

Third-party components
----------------------
No third-party libraries, fonts or datasets are bundled. The app is plain
HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no build step and no runtime dependencies.

One third-party request is made at runtime: a cookieless page-view counter
(GoatCounter) loaded from gc.zgo.at and reporting to a self-hosted endpoint,
scoped to a single aggregate path. It sets no cookies, stores no personal data
and performs no cross-site tracking. Fork authors who do not want it can delete
the script tag near the end of index.html — nothing depends on it.

All user state — answers, progress, selected Bundesland — is held in
localStorage on the user's own device and never leaves the browser. The service
worker cache is likewise local. Only anonymous aggregate page views are
transmitted.

Contributions
-------------
Unless a contribution states otherwise, contributions are accepted under the
same split: code as MIT, own written content as CC BY-SA 4.0. Contributions to
the question texts must reproduce the official catalogue verbatim rather than
rephrasing it, so the material stays identical to the statutory text.
